# Analyses

Links above Total impact switch the results between analyses
(`useUI().analysis`, transient). Every analysis runs from the same plan; an
analysis only adds options to `calculate(household, refs, options)`. The
conversion schedule always comes from the plan's strategy as-is.

- **Retirement**: `calculate(household, refs)`, no options. Golden cases pin it.
- **Survivorship**: `options.death` = `survivorshipEvent(household)`: the saved
  `household.survivorship` (person + age, set in the tab) or the default (main
  person at 80). Needs two people.
- **Long-term care**: `options.longTermCare` = `longTermCareSettings(household)`
  (saved `household.longTermCare`, healed on read; default: main person, home
  care, 3%, starting at 80).
- **Disability**: `options.disability` = `disabilityEvent(household, start)`
  (saved `household.disability`, person + age; default: main person
  disabled this year). It can start before the plan start, like Survivorship. From January 1 of that year the
  person's salary and business income stop; their disability policies pay
  (see the insurance guide). Expenses don't change.

- **Mortgage analysis**: not a projection. `MortgageAnalysis.tsx` edits
  `household.mortgages` (statement fields) and shows `amortize()`
  (`lib/engine/amortization.ts`): monthly interest = balance x rate / 12 to
  the cent, payment left at 0 is worked out from the maturity date, a
  payment on the maturity date clears cent rounding, extra principal shows
  its savings. It never feeds `projectScenario`. Every mortgage entered on a
  property (`RealEstate.loans`, kind mortgage, balance > 0) with no statement
  linked by `loanId` (saved or deleted) shows up as one
  (`propertyMortgageStatements`); the first edit saves it, Remove saves then
  deletes it so it stays gone. ResultsView returns it before the tax tables load.

- **Social Security analysis**: not a projection. `SocialSecurityAnalysis.tsx`
  edits `household.socialSecurityClaims` (person, claiming age, monthly
  benefit at that age; at most `MAX_SOCIAL_SECURITY_CLAIMS`, 2, per person,
  the first two entered) and shows `socialSecurityBreakeven()`
  (`lib/engine/socialSecurity.ts`). The first option is the plan's own Social
  Security income (its start year and amount); every option grows by that
  income's COLA (`growthRate`) from its own claim year, full years, before
  tax, not discounted, through the person's final age. The crossover year is
  the first year an option's running total reaches the plan's (from either
  side). It never feeds `projectScenario`; ResultsView returns it before the
  tax tables load.

- **Car analysis**: not a projection. `CarAnalysis.tsx` edits
  `household.carDecision` and shows `compareCarOptions()`
  (`lib/engine/carDecision.ts`): pay cash, finance (down payment, APR, term)
  or lease (monthly, term, due at signing, turn-in fee) over `years`. Bought
  cars depreciate `depreciationRate` a year and count their value less any
  loan owed at the end; a lease shorter than the comparison is renewed on the
  same terms, and one still running at the end counts only its months
  inside. Lease mileage: miles over `leaseMilesPerYear` (from
  `milesDrivenPerYear`) cost `excessMileRate` each, paid at each turn-in
  (prorated for a lease still running). The tables color each option (cash
  blue, finance rust, lease green). "Net cost with lost growth" carries every payment forward at
  `investmentReturn`. Defaults (`defaultCarDecision`) take the price from a
  one-time depreciation entry named like a car. Before income tax.

## Survivorship rules (`domain/survivorship.ts` + `projectScenario`)

A death (and, in Disability, a disability) can be any year from today on;
the lists read "2028 · age 55". When the event comes before the plan start
(first retirement year), `analysisStartYear` starts the projection in that
year (never before `asOfDate`'s year) and runs to the same final
age. Start-year amounts (incomes, expenses, rent, care) stay anchored to the
plan start: before it they hold at the entered amount. The conversion
schedule is per plan year, so earlier years convert nothing. Accounts,
property and business values compound from today to the earlier start;
mortgages amortize from it. ResultsView keys the snapshot by the first row's
year so Jump to resets.

The person passes at the end of `birthYear + deathAge`; effects start the
next year:
- filing status mfj becomes single (row `filingStatus`), senior deduction and
  Medicare count only `livingIds`,
- life-only pensions of the deceased stop, survivorship pensions keep paying
  the same amount (`pensionPayout`),
- Social Security: the survivor keeps the larger of the two benefits; the
  tab notes a step-up (`socialSecurityAfterDeath`) or that none happens,
- the deceased's salary, business and disability insurance income stop;
  withdrawals and other income keep running,
- accounts pass to the survivor (`effectiveOwner`): RMDs and conversion
  eligibility follow the survivor's age.
- expenses drop to `SURVIVOR_EXPENSE_SHARE` (70%) of the plan's amounts.
Inheritance uses the final row's filing status.

## Lifetime income from the accounts once an event happens

Survivorship (from the year after the death) and Long-term care (from the
first care year) turn every investment and retirement account (after-tax,
tax-deferred, Roth; never business or real estate) into a fixed lifetime
income, the same way life insurance proceeds work: the balance that
January times `lifeInsurancePayoutRate` (the Transamerica "Immediate
Payout %" column; under 59 is 5%, past 86 holds) for the age of the person
it pays (the owner, the survivor after a death, the younger spouse on a
joint account). From then the account grows 8%
(`LIFE_INSURANCE_PROCEEDS_GROWTH`) instead of its own rate and pays the
income first (step 2b). Guaranteed: past an empty balance it keeps paying,
taxed like the account's money (tax-deferred ordinary, after-tax as gains
vs basis, Roth free). The income counts toward the RMD: only an RMD above it
is added. Income line `draw:<accountId>` (`eventWithdrawalKey`), labelled
"Lifetime income · <account>", charted as Withdrawals from assets.

A year still short after that income (step 5c) takes more from the same
accounts until the surplus is 0 or they're empty: after-tax first, then
tax-deferred, then Roth, grossed up for the tax it adds (fixed point on
`priceYear`; the rental-loss allowance keeps its pre-draw MAGI). Line
`topup:<accountId>` (`eventTopUpKey`), "Extra withdrawal · <account>". A
year with more income than spending keeps the surplus. Disability doesn't
do any of this (yet).

Survivorship, Disability and Long-term care show only the no-conversion
scenario (`single` in ResultsView): one snapshot table, no No/With toggles,
no conversion tax switch, and Summary shows `ScenarioMetrics` (the six totals)
instead of Total impact. Retirement still compares the two.

## Long-term care rules (`domain/longTermCare.ts` + `projectScenario`)

- One spouse or both. Each person in care has a start age (default 80) and a
  length in years (default 3 for men, 5 for women, 3 when `Person.sex` isn't
  set), both editable in the tab.
- Cost per person, chosen per person (`CarePeriod.careType`, falling back to
  the settings' `careType` for older plans): home care $6,000/mo or nursing home $10,000/mo in
  first-projection-year dollars, grown by `inflation` (default 3%) each year.
  It lands in `expenseMonthly` under `ltc:<personId>` (labelled by
  `expenseLabel`) and in `monthlyExpenses`.
- Regular expenses: 70% while some of the household is in care, 0 while
  everyone is, back to 100% after care ends. Survivorship's 70% multiplies in
  if both analyses' options are ever combined.
- The tab opens with a note saying so (the user asked for it on top).
- Care costs aren't deducted as medical expenses (no itemizing in the model).

## Results layout (what clients see first)

Approved by the user: keep it (see AGENTS.md, "Results layout").

Under the analysis links, four tabs (`ResultsView` state, Main on load);
each tab shows only its own sections:
- **Main**: the Assets, Income & Taxes tables.
- **Summary**: Total impact, the metric cards, and After-tax assets. Taxes
  show as lifetime only; the before and after RMD age split opens from a
  "Show taxes before and after" link under the cards.
- **Graphic view**: `PresentationCharts` plus the Assets / Cash flow charts.
- **Main in detail analysis**: the Year-by-year table, alone in the scroller
  for pin-to-top.
The No conversion / With conversion toggle sits on Year-by-year and on the
Assets and cash flow charts (shared state).

## Jump to from today

ResultsView runs the plan twice: the normal run (Summary totals) and a
display run with `options.fromYear` = this year for Main, Graphic view and
Year-by-year, so Jump to reaches back to today. Amounts, property and
business values stay anchored to the plan start (before it they hold at
today's values), conversions only run in plan years, so from the plan start
on the display run matches the normal one (engine test pins it; mortgages
amortize from the earlier year). The tables still open on the plan start.
